(Timeline \(UTC\)) Tj T*
T*
(14:02 A large customer endpoint starts returning HTTP 503 for every request.) Tj T*
(14:06 Delivery throughput in prod-us2 drops sharply. WL-ALERT-QLAG fires and pages the primary) Tj T*
(on-call engineer.) Tj T*
(14:11 The primary scales up delivery workers. Lag keeps growing because the new workers also) Tj T*
(block on the pool.) Tj T*
(14:19 SEV1 declared and an incident commander assigned. Status page updated at 14:24.) Tj T*
(14:38 The team finds hundreds of idle-in-transaction connections owned by the retry scheduler.) Tj T*
(14:44 The retry scheduler is paused. Connections are released and workers recover.) Tj T*
(14:53 Delivery lag back to normal. Retry scheduler resumed with a hotfix.) Tj T*
T*
(What went well) Tj T*
T*
(Publishing was isolated from delivery, so customers could keep sending events. Pausing the) Tj T*
(retry scheduler was possible without a deploy.) Tj T*
T*
(What went poorly) Tj T*
T*
(Nothing alerted on connection pool saturation; we found it by reading database sessions by) Tj T*
(hand. Scaling up workers made the contention worse, which cost about 20 minutes.) Tj T*
T*
(Action items) Tj T*
T*
(ACT-112: Lower db.pool.acquire_timeout_ms from 30000 to 2000 so workers fail fast. Owner: Tomas) Tj T*
(Lindqvist. Done in 3.5.1.) Tj T*
(ACT-113: Add an alert when database connections exceed 90% of the pool. Owner: Priya Raman.) Tj T*
(Done, see WL-ALERT-PGCONN.) Tj T*
(ACT-114: Load test the retry scheduler with 100,000 pending retries before each release. Owner:) Tj T*
(Delivery team. Open.) Tj T*
(ACT-115: Reschedule retries in batched updates instead of one transaction per delivery. Owner:) Tj T*
(Delivery team. Done in 3.5.1.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
